        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-oriented-link-in-s-three-and-ambient-isotopy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Equivalence of links.** Two oriented links $L_0\\colon C_0\\to S^3$ and\n$L_1\\colon C_1\\to S^3$ are **equivalent** (written $L_0\\sim L_1$), or\n**ambient isotopic**, when there are an ambient isotopy $H$ and an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism $f:C_0\\to C_1$ with\n$H_1\\circ L_0=L_1\\circ f$. Thus parametrizations and component labels are\nirrelevant, while the induced orientation of each image component is preserved. The relation is an equivalence relation on links:\nit is reflexive with $H=\\mathrm{id}$, transitive by composing isotopies, and\nsymmetric because a smooth family of diffeomorphisms has a smooth family of\ninverses. An equivalence class is an **(oriented) link type**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-knot-connected-sum-and-prime-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a coordinate orientation-reversing reflection $r:S^3\\to S^3$ for the mirror convention. Let $K$ and $J$ be smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented three-sphere. A connected-sum datum consists of one smoothly embedded closed three-ball around a short subarc of $K$ and one around a short subarc of $J$, each meeting its knot in a single trivial properly embedded arc, together with the fixed standard orientation-reversing boundary identification of the two standard oriented ball boundaries that joins the oriented arcs into one circle. Choose this standard map once in the standard two-ball model so that it commutes with $r$ and an orientation-preserving half-turn interchanging the two balls preserves the joining convention. Here a trivial arc is an embedded interval in a ball that is carried by an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of ball-arc pairs to a straight diameter. Remove the interiors of the two balls and glue the remaining boundary spheres using this fixed identification; the resulting oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere is a connected sum of $K$ and $J$, denoted $K\\#J$ for the specified datum.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
